  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 157 AM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nOn Sunday, an upper trough and closed low will be located over the \nCentral Canadian Provinces with an upper trough extending \nsouthwest across the Great Basin into central California. This \nmay set up a frontal boundary across southeast Montana into \nsouthern North Dakota. Surface high pressure will be in place \nacross the Great Lakes into the Central Plains. Mild \ntemperatures in the upper 70s to lower 80s across western \nNebraska, while eastern areas only reach the low 70s. The \nfrontal boundary will begin to drop south as a cold front \nacross the forecast area Monday night into Tuesday. The \nadvancement of a slow moving longwave trough and cold frontal \npassage will bring increasing chances for showers and storms \nSunday night in the west, and Monday into Monday night. Likely \nPOPS Monday night. Chance POPS for showers linger Tuesday into \nTuesday night. There remain timing differences in the cold front\nand upper trough. The deterministic GFS is faster, progressing \nthe upper trough axis through western Nebraska on Tuesday. The \nECMWF is much slower and supports a more lengthy potential for \nprecipitation chances through at least Wednesday, as it \ndevelops a closed low east from the Four Corners Tuesday night \ninto Wednesday. Therefore, confidence in timing and duration is\nbelow average.\n\nHighs Monday in the low 70s, to upper 70s far southwest should \ncool into the 60s Tuesday and Wednesday.",
